\title{openreadout: read raw laboratory instrument files}
\description{
Reads raw files from microscopes (Zeiss CZI, Nikon ND2, Leica LIF, Olympus, whole-slide
images, OME-TIFF, OME-Zarr, ...), flow cytometers (FCS), mass spectrometers (Thermo \code{.raw},
mzML, Bruker timsTOF, Agilent, Waters, Sciex), chromatographs (ChemStation, ANDI, Shimadzu),
plate readers, qPCR cyclers, electrophysiology rigs and NMR spectrometers into R arrays and data
frames, without vendor software. The readers are the OpenReadout Rust library, compiled into
the package.
}
\details{
The functions are named after the commands of the \code{openreadout} command-line tool:
\itemize{
\item Metadata: \code{\link[=openreadout_info]{openreadout_info()}} (\code{view = "summary"}, \code{"full"}, \code{"structure"}, \code{"explain"}
or \code{"format"}), \code{\link[=openreadout_check]{openreadout_check()}}, \code{\link[=openreadout_formats]{openreadout_formats()}}, \code{\link[=openreadout_ome_xml]{openreadout_ome_xml()}}.
\item Images: \code{\link[=openreadout_read_image]{openreadout_read_image()}}, \code{\link[=openreadout_read_plane]{openreadout_read_plane()}}, \code{\link[=openreadout_levels]{openreadout_levels()}},
\code{\link[=openreadout_stats]{openreadout_stats()}}.
\item Tables and signals: \code{\link[=openreadout_table]{openreadout_table()}}, \code{\link[=openreadout_trace]{openreadout_trace()}}, \code{\link[=openreadout_scans]{openreadout_scans()}}, \code{\link[=openreadout_spectrum]{openreadout_spectrum()}}.
\item Analyses: \code{\link[=openreadout_analyze]{openreadout_analyze()}} (chromatograms, peaks, NMR peaks, patch-clamp
